riscv: add a Linux boot image header

Put the RISC-V Linux "Image" header in the first 64 bytes of the kernel,
with a jump to _alt_start as its first instruction. A flat binary of the
kernel (objcopy -O binary) can then be booted by firmware and boot loaders
that only understand the Linux format, such as the Sedna board firmware
(sedna-fw) or U-Boot's booti. Firmware that jumps to the load address
without parsing the header, like OpenSBI fw_jump, keeps working.

text_offset is 2MB: locore maps the kernel with 2MB superpages, so it must
be loaded at a 2MB aligned address.

diff --git a/sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S b/sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S
index 305ed8d79f10..2a3a2c459bf3 100644
--- a/sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S
+++ b/sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S
@@ -52,9 +52,38 @@
 	.set	kernbase, KERNBASE
 
 	.text
+/*
+ * RISC-V Linux boot image header (Documentation/arch/riscv/boot-image-header.rst
+ * in Linux). It occupies the first 64 bytes of the kernel, so a flat binary of
+ * the kernel (objcopy -O binary) can be booted by firmware and boot loaders
+ * that only understand the Linux "Image" format. Entering at the start of the
+ * image jumps straight to _alt_start, so firmware that just jumps to the load
+ * address without parsing the header (e.g. OpenSBI fw_jump) keeps working.
+ *
+ * The kernel must be loaded at a 2MB aligned physical address, see the
+ * superpage mappings built in pagetables below; text_offset says so.
+ */
+	.globl	linux_image_header
+linux_image_header:
+.option push
+.option norvc
+	j	_alt_start			/* code0: must be 4 bytes */
+.option pop
+	.word	0				/* code1 */
+	.quad	0x200000			/* text_offset from start of RAM */
+	.quad	_end - KERNBASE			/* image_size, including bss */
+	.quad	0				/* flags: little endian */
+	.word	0x2				/* version 0.2 */
+	.word	0				/* res1 */
+	.quad	0				/* res2 */
+	.ascii	"RISCV\0\0\0"			/* magic (deprecated) */
+	.ascii	"RSC\x05"			/* magic2 */
+	.word	0				/* res3 */
+
 /*
  * Alternate entry point. Used when booting via SBI firmware. It must be placed
- * at the beginning of the .text section. Arguments are as follows:
+ * right after the Linux image header, at the beginning of the .text section.
+ * Arguments are as follows:
  *  - a0 = hart ID
  *  - a1 = dtbp
  *
